  "enrichment": {
    "workspace_path": "C:\\a\\1\\s is the default working directory of a self-hosted Azure Pipelines agent: 'a' is the agent work root, '1' is the agent's numeric work folder for this pipeline, and 's' is the sources directory. The short path is chosen by the agent to avoid Windows MAX_PATH limits during builds. The detection allowlist covers \\source\\repos and Program Files but not agent work roots, which is why this fired.",
    "process_lineage": "The chain is Agent.Listener.exe (running as a service) -> Agent.Worker.exe -> cmd.exe -> MSBuild.exe. Agent.Listener is the long-lived agent service; Agent.Worker is the per-job process it forks. There is no remote-execution parent anywhere in the chain.",
    "source_provenance": "The project file MSBuild consumed was created 0.7 seconds earlier by git.exe, running under the same agent worker, as part of 'git checkout --progress --force 3f9c1a7d\u2026'. Its provenance is a source-control checkout of a specific commit, not a file placed on the host by another machine.",
    "network_context": "The only outbound connection from the agent is to dev.azure.com on TCP/443 \u2014 the pipeline service the agent polls for work. No connection to an unrecognised address appears in the window.",
    "compilation_artifacts": "MSBuild spawns csc.exe and cvtres.exe and writes .cs, .dll, .pdb and .cmdline files under the project's own obj\\Release directory. Compiler invocation is not by itself suspicious: it is what a build is. What matters is that the outputs land in the project tree, named after the project, rather than in randomly-named directories under a user's Temp folder.",
